<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Río de la Plata</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Río de la Plata is the broad estuary formed by the Paraná and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> rivers on the border of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and Uruguay, and by extension the surrounding region. Control of its eastern bank was contested between Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and Spanish, later Argentine, authorities. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a> note that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Emperor Pedro I</a>'s ambitions there led to a costly war with Argentina over the territory that became Uruguay.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata.jpg" alt="Río de la Plata" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Earth Sciences and Image Analysis Laboratory, NASA Johnson Space Center · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rio_de_la_Plata_BA_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Río de la Plata, in English the River Plate, is the estuary formed by the confluence of the Uruguay River and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parana-river">Paraná River</a> at Punta Gorda. It flows east into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> in a funnel-shaped indentation on the south-eastern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. It is about 290 km long and widens from about 2 km at its head to about 220 km at its mouth. Geographers disagree whether to call it a river, an estuary, a gulf or a marginal sea. Its drainage basin covers about a quarter of the continent. Part of the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and Uruguay runs along it, and its coasts hold the port cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Nomadic aboriginal peoples lived in the region for thousands of years before European settlers arrived. The Portuguese first explored the estuary in 1512–13. In 1516 the Spanish navigator Juan Díaz de Solís crossed it in search of a passage between the Atlantic and the Pacific. Sebastian Cabot explored the Paraná and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> rivers between 1526 and 1529 and obtained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> trinkets from the Guaraní, which, with legends of a Sierra de la Plata, led him to rename it Río de la Plata, the river of silver. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-mendoza">Pedro de Mendoza</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> on 2 February 1536, but the settlement was soon abandoned, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-garay">Juan de Garay</a> refounded it on 11 June 1580.</p><p>The region became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> of the Río de la Plata. Buenos Aires became a centre of trade outside the Spanish fleet system, exporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potosi">Potosí</a> and importing European goods, slaves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>. In 1776 the Bourbon Crown raised the governorate to the Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata. A British invasion in 1806 and 1807 was defeated by the local garrison and population.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After independence in the early nineteenth century, interests in the territories and navigation rights on the river played a major part in many conflicts, including the Cisplatine and Platine wars and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguayan-war">Paraguayan War</a>. Brazil blockaded the river from 1826 to 1828, the French from 1838 to 1840 and an Anglo-French alliance from 1845 to 1850.</p><p>In December 1939 the German pocket battleship Admiral Graf Spee fought British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> cruisers off the estuary in the first naval battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. She put into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montevideo">Montevideo</a>, where her captain scuttled her a few days later.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Río de la Plata” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His ambitions in the <b>Río de la Plata</b> produced the costly Cisplatine War (1825–1828), which ended with the independence of Uruguay.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 25 August 1825 the Thirty-Three Orientals under Juan Antonio Lavalleja declared independence with the backing of the United Provinces of the <b>Río de la Plata</b>, and the 500-day Cisplatine War followed between Brazil and the United Provinces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Oriental Republic of Uruguay · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Upper Peru had been transferred from the Viceroyalty of Peru to the new Viceroyalty of the <b>Río de la Plata</b> in 1776, so both Lima and Buenos Aires claimed it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-bolivia">Republic of Bolivia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The city’s legacy is visible in the palaces of the Strada Nuova and the Rolli system of lodging state guests, now a World Heritage Site, in its Baroque art, and in Genoese communities that shaped Gibraltar, Corsica, Sardinia’s Carloforte and the <b>Río de la Plata</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Paraguay grew out of the Spanish province governed from Asunción, founded in 1537, whose largely Guaraní-speaking mestizo population lived far from the centres of empire; from 1776 it belonged to the Viceroyalty of the <b>Río de la Plata</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-paraguay">Republic of Paraguay · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…(1535) and Peru (1542) were the primary administrative units, later joined by New Granada (1717, re-established 1739) and <b>Río de la Plata</b> (1776), each headed by a viceroy of high rank who exercised near-royal authority subject to the oversight of the audiencias (colonial high courts) and the Council…</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
